#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068} :activity/id #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163326 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$, then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$. Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X^(―)] &= Var[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{n})^{2} \\cdot Var[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot Var[X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot (Var[X_{1}] + Var[X_{2}] + ⋯ + Var[X_{n}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot (σ^{2} + σ^{2} + ⋯ + σ^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot nσ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$. What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6276 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" :step/math-academy-id 38034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162380}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" :step/math-academy-id 38036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" :step/math-academy-id 38037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410430}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"} :step/id #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" :step/math-academy-id 38040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163333 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$, then the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6300 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" :step/math-academy-id 38149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163334 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. We know that the sample mean\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$.\n\nHowever, it should be noted that each $X_i$ is *also* an unbiased estimator of the population mean! Recall that, by assumption\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nIn what sense is $\\overline{X}$ a \"better\" estimator of $\\mu$ than, say, $X_1$? The answer lies in their respective variances.\n\nAs the sample size $n$ becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of $\\overline{X}$ become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as $n\\to\\infty$,\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\to 0, \\qquad \\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\to 0\n$$\n\nSo, when the sample size $n$ is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$. Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that $\\overline{X}$ will give good estimates for $\\mu$. The individual sample elements do not have this property since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean $\\mu$ as the sample size $n$ increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6301 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean and Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" :step/math-academy-id 38150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162169}]}
